I wanted to tell you a little bit about  universals restoration program and a  little bit about this film and as part  of our Universal Centennial in 2012  celebration we announced an expanded  effort to restore the universal film  library since then we have fully  restored 50 titles which includes some  of our classics All Quiet on the Western  Front the birds buck privates Dracula  and Frankenstein at Africa the sting  pillow talk to kill a mockingbird Touch  of Evil Double Indemnity Holiday Inn and  Spartacus just name a few in 2015 we  announced our silent film initiative  which was to restore some of the world's  most classic silent films such as the  last warning outside the law Oh dr.  sensation seekers and many more that  we're working on Universal is committed  to restoring even more films in the  years to come we are in constant  conversation with film archives  festivals and scholars from around the  world there are certain films that come  up all the time  tonight's film is one of those films  king of jazz is special for a number of  reasons at its heart the film is a  lavish musical showcase for Paul  Whiteman and his orchestra in universals  history this is one of the biggest  productions of the time and they're in  an Academy Award for best art direction  the film introduces a 27 year old Bing  Crosby in one of his first feature film  roles king of jazz is also one of those  surviving to color Technicolor films in  the world the film was first released in  1930 and then re-released as a shorter  version in 1933 our goal was to restored  the original version 1930 which ran 105  minutes we started with the soundtrack  negative from the universal vaults that  matched the 105 minute version this  soundtrack served as our template to  which we can form  and four different picture elements used  for the restoration about 60% of the  picture came from the original  Technicolor to color negative that was  held by Universal we undertook a  worldwide search for the elements to  fill in the missing picture most of the  remaining 40% of the picture comes from  vintage 35 millimeter prints that were  held by the Cohan film collection and  the Packard humanities Institute at the  UCLA film and television archive one  hard-to-find sketch was discovered on a  print that was obtained from the Danish  Film Institute in order to retain such  as much of the sound as possible any  remaining shots that we're missing came  from stills that came from our Universal  archives Group one of the film once the  film was digitally scanned and the  structure of the restoration was locked  univer Universal restoration team  performed color correction dirt removal  scratch repair and image stabilization  working in a high resolution 4k workflow  in addition the digital audio  restoration was conducted using both the  1930 and the 1933 nitrate optical  soundtrack negatives both picture and  sound restoration work was performed at  NBC Universal Studio Post which is our  internal facility on the universal lot  in addition to using our in-house  restoration team we also consulted with  experts in the field David Pierce and  James Layton and because every  restoration is a collaborative effort  we'd also like to recognize ycm labs  sonarak &amp; Prasad who also assisted with  film evaluation scanning and recombining  them some of the images and finally I  would like thank Randy and the academy  for hosting the West Coast premiere of  the restoration of the king of jazz we  all hope that you enjoy it thank you
 

